aggerated claims about the use of AI, with the US Federal Trade Commission having underscored its focus on \"ensuring the promise of new technology isn't misused as a means to mislead consumers.\"\n\n### AI and the \"Truth Crisis\"\nConcerns are growing about an era of \"truth decay\" where AI content dupes us, shapes our beliefs even when we catch the lie, and erodes societal trust in the process. The US Department of Homeland Security is now using AI video generators from Google and Adobe to make content shared with the public, as immigration agencies have flooded social media with content to support mass deportation efforts.\n\n### Major Copyright Lawsuits\nLawsuits against AI giants OpenAI, Anthropic, and Perplexity are set to continue headlining intellectual property developments in California federal courts in 2026. Writers including Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist John Carreyrou filed a copyright lawsuit accusing six AI giants of using pirated copies of their books to train large language models, claiming Anthropic, Google, OpenAI, Meta Platforms, xAI and Perplexity committed a \"deliberate act of theft.\"\n\n### Google-Israel Military AI Controversy\nA whistleblower complaint filed with the SEC claims that Google breached its own ethics rules to help an Israeli contractor apply AI to drone surveillance video.\n\n### AI Chatbot Controversies\nIn early 2026, Elon Musk's AI chatbot Grok became the center of a global firestorm following an update to its image-generation model, Aurora, which allowed users to manipulate photographs of real people.\n\nThese controversies highlight ongoing concerns about AI ethics, intellectual property rights, government transparency, and the potential for AI to be used for misleading claims or harmful purposes."],"durationSeconds":20.775191200000002}}
[2026-02-04T00:03:09.290Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MvHDs7Q4tifQ1K9v7Tg9ER","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Trump tariffs trade policy 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Trump Tariffs: The Economic Impact of the Trump Trade War\",\"url\":\"https://taxfoundation.org/research/all/federal/trump-tariffs-trade-war/\"},{\"title\":\"TPC Tariff Tracker | Tax Policy Center\",\"url\":\"https://taxpolicycenter.org/features/tracking-trump-tariffs\"},{\"title\":\"Fact Sheet: President Donald J. Trump Takes Action on Certain Advanced Computing Chips to Protect America’s Economic and National Security\",\"url\":\"https://www.whitehouse.gov/fact-sheets/2026/01/fact-sheet-president-donald-j-trump-takes-action-on-certain-advanced-computing-chips-to-protect-americas-economic-and-national-security/\"},{\"title\":\"Presidential 2025 Tariff Actions: Timeline and Status | Congress.gov | Library of Congress\",\"url\":\"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48549\"},{\"title\":\"US Tariffs: What’s the Impact? | J.P. Morgan Global Research\",\"url\":\"https://www.jpmorgan.com/insights/global-research/current-events/us-tariffs\"},{\"title\":\"Trump 2.0 tariff tracker | Trade Compliance Resource Hub\",\"url\":\"https://www.tradecomplianceresourcehub.com/2026/02/02/trump-2-0-tariff-tracker/\"},{\"title\":\"Trump's trade war wreaked little havoc on trade patterns last year | PIIE\",\"url\":\"https://www.piie.com/blogs/realtime-economics/2026/trumps-trade-war-wreaked-little-havoc-trade-patterns-last-year\"},{\"title\":\"Why Trump's Tariffs Are Like Termites | TIME\",\"url\":\"https://time.com/collections/davos-2026/7339213/trump-tariffs-inflation-us-economy-global-trade-impact/\"},{\"title\":\"Tariffs in the second Trump administration - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration\"},{\"title\":\"Tracking the impact of Trump’s tariff policy - CNN\",\"url\":\"https://edition.cnn.com/interactive/2026/business/tracking-trump-tariff-impact-us/\"}]\n\nHere's an overview of Trump's tariffs and trade policy in 2026 based on the latest information:\n\n## Current Tariff Levels\n\nPresident Donald Trump has raised tariffs to the highest level in nearly a century. Before Trump took office, the US average effective tariff rate was 2.4%, according to estimates from the Yale Budget Lab. At the end of 2025, it was 16.8%.\n\nAccording to the Tax Policy Center, the average tariff rate on all imports is currently 17 percent. If all announced policies take effect, the average tariff rate on all imported goods will rise to 21 percent.\n\n## Economic Impact\n\nThe Trump tariffs are the largest US tax increase as a percent of GDP (0.55 percent for 2026) since 1993. The tariffs amount to an average tax increase per US household of $1,000 in 2025 and $1,300 in 2026.\n\nTPC estimates that tariffs will raise about $2.3 trillion in fiscal years 2026 through 2035, with $247 billion raised in 2026.\n\nThe tariffs have brought in record amounts of revenue for the government, but they've come at a cost: Consumers are paying higher prices for many essentials and employers have been increasingly reluctant to take on more workers.\n\n## Key Trade Developments\n\n- In November 2025, the U.S. and China reached an agreement to extend tariff reductions for one year, through November 10, 2026.\n- On January 15, the Commerce Department unveiled a new trade agreement with Taiwan that reduces reciprocal and Section 232 tariffs on Taiwanese-origin imports and creates firm-specific semiconductor tariff exemptions for those that invest in US production capacity.\n- On January 21, 2026, Trump said he wouldn't impose tariffs on several European nations after he reached the \"framework of a future deal\" on Greenland and the Arctic region with NATO Secretary-General Mark Rutte.\n- The Trump administration has signaled that tariffs on pharmaceuticals could potentially rise toward 200% by mid- to late-2026.\n\n## Legal Challenges\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court is currently evaluating the legality of President Trump's decision to impose sweeping tariffs under the International Economic Emergency Powers Act (IEEPA), and a ruling is expected in early 2026. If the Court declares the tariffs unlawful, the Trump administration could lose the authority to collect duties and might need to return revenues already received.\n\nOn August 29, the Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit affirmed the Court of International Trade's holding that \"fentanyl\" and reciprocal tariffs exceed the President's authority under IEEPA. The Federal Circuit stayed its ruling pending further appeal by the government. The Supreme Court heard oral argument in the pending appeal on November 5.\n\n## Global Trade Impact\n\nPerhaps surprisingly, there has been very little reconfiguration of global trade, at least through October of 2025. Two-way trade with the United States as a share of total trade changed very little among 19 impacted foreign partners. However, trade shares in 2026 could tell a different story.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc19be83-c5d4-4d22-a1ea-f9f460ee03af","uuid":"eb719a18-b1ad-47ec-aed8-cdeee9ad4138","tool_use_result":{"query":"Trump tariffs trade policy 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01UdsrqVQp3RppdCdozyM9Kz","content":[{"title":"Trump Tariffs: The Economic Impact of the Trump Trade War","url":"https://taxfoundation.org/research/all/federal/trump-tariffs-trade-war/"},{"title":"TPC Tariff Tracker | Tax Policy Center","url":"https://taxpolicycenter.org/features/tracking-trump-tariffs"},{"title":"Fact Sheet: President Donald J. Trump Takes Action on Certain Advanced Computing Chips to Protect America’s Economic and National Security","url":"https://www.whitehouse.gov/fact-sheets/2026/01/fact-sheet-president-donald-j-trump-takes-action-on-certain-advanced-computing-chips-to-protect-americas-economic-and-national-security/"},{"title":"Presidential 2025 Tariff Actions: Timeline and Status | Congress.gov | Library of Congress","url":"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48549"},{"title":"US Tariffs: What’s the Impact? | J.P. Morgan Global Research","url":"https://www.jpmorgan.com/insights/global-research/current-events/us-tariffs"},{"title":"Trump 2.0 tariff tracker | Trade Compliance Resource Hub","url":"https://www.tradecomplianceresourcehub.com/2026/02/02/trump-2-0-tariff-tracker/"},{"title":"Trump's trade war wreaked little havoc on trade patterns last year | PIIE","url":"https://www.piie.com/blogs/realtime-economics/2026/trumps-trade-war-wreaked-little-havoc-trade-patterns-last-year"},{"title":"Why Trump's Tariffs Are Like Termites | TIME","url":"https://time.com/collections/davos-2026/7339213/trump-tariffs-inflation-us-economy-global-trade-impact/"},{"title":"Tariffs in the second Trump administration - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration"},{"title":"Tracking the impact of Trump’s tariff policy - CNN","url":"https://edition.cnn.com/interactive/2026/business/tracking-trump-tariff-impact-us/"}]},"Here's an overview of Trump's tariffs and trade policy in 2026 based on the latest information:\n\n## Current Tariff Levels\n\nPresident Donald Trump has raised tariffs to the highest level in nearly a century. Before Trump took office, the US average effective tariff rate was 2.4%, according to estimates from the Yale Budget Lab. At the end of 2025, it was 16.8%.\n\nAccording to the Tax Policy Center, the average tariff rate on all imports is currently 17 percent. If all announced policies take effect, the average tariff rate on all imported goods will rise to 21 percent.\n\n## Economic Impact\n\nThe Trump tariffs are the largest US tax increase as a percent of GDP (0.55 percent for 2026) since 1993. The tariffs amount to an average tax increase per US household of $1,000 in 2025 and $1,300 in 2026.\n\nTPC estimates that tariffs will raise about $2.3 trillion in fiscal years 2026 through 2035, with $247 billion raised in 2026.\n\nThe tariffs have brought in record amounts of revenue for the government, but they've come at a cost: Consumers are paying higher prices for many essentials and employers have been increasingly reluctant to take on more workers.\n\n## Key Trade Developments\n\n- In November 2025, the U.S. and China reached an agreement to extend tariff reductions for one year, through November 10, 2026.\n- On January 15, the Commerce Department unveiled a new trade agreement with Taiwan that reduces reciprocal and Section 232 tariffs on Taiwanese-origin imports and creates firm-specific semiconductor tariff exemptions for those that invest in US production capacity.\n- On January 21, 2026, Trump said he wouldn't impose tariffs on several European nations after he reached the \"framework of a future deal\" on Greenland and the Arctic region with NATO Secretary-General Mark Rutte.\n- The Trump administration has signaled that tariffs on pharmaceuticals could potentially rise toward 200% by mid- to late-2026.\n\n## Legal Challenges\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court is currently evaluating the legality of President Trump's decision to impose sweeping tariffs under the International Economic Emergency Powers Act (IEEPA), and a ruling is expected in early 2026. If the Court declares the tariffs unlawful, the Trump administration could lose the authority to collect duties and might need to return revenues already received.\n\nOn August 29, the Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit affirmed the Court of International Trade's holding that \"fentanyl\" and reciprocal tariffs exceed the President's authority under IEEPA. The Federal Circuit stayed its ruling pending further appeal by the government. The Supreme Court heard oral argument in the pending appeal on November 5.\n\n## Global Trade Impact\n\nPerhaps surprisingly, there has been very little reconfiguration of global trade, at least through October of 2025. Two-way trade with the United States as a share of total trade changed very little among 19 impacted foreign partners. However, trade shares in 2026 could tell a different story."],"durationSeconds":21.572456}}
[2026-02-04T00:03:10.098Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019svL5fQW1R9Eyegb4Jb5Am","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"tech layoffs companies 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | FOX 10 Phoenix\",\"url\":\"https://www.fox10phoenix.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year\"},{\"title\":\"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | FOX 4 Dallas-Fort Worth\",\"url\":\"https://www.fox4news.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year\"},{\"title\":\"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | FOX 11 Los Angeles\",\"url\":\"https://www.foxla.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year\"},{\"title\":\"Layoffs Tracker - All Tech and Startup Layoffs\",\"url\":\"https://www.trueup.io/layoffs\"},{\"title\":\"2026 tech company layoffs\",\"url\":\"https://www.informationweek.com/it-staffing-careers/2026-tech-company-layoffs\"},{\"title\":\"Amazon’s layoffs are staggering. We’ve seen this before\",\"url\":\"https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/31/tech/amazon-layoffs-january-tech-changes\"},{\"title\":\"Tech layoffs roundup: Every major company cutting jobs in 2025-2026\",\"url\":\"https://www.androidheadlines.com/2026/01/tech-layoffs-roundup-every-major-company-cutting-jobs-in-2025-2026.html\"},{\"title\":\"One month into 2026, global tech and startup layoffs near 25,000\",\"url\":\"https://www.businesstoday.in/technology/news/story/one-month-into-2026-global-tech-and-startup-layoffs-near-25000-514445-2026-02-03\"},{\"title\":\"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | LiveNOW from FOX\",\"url\":\"https://www.livenowfox.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year\"},{\"title\":\"List of companies laying off employees in February - Newsweek\",\"url\":\"https://www.newsweek.com/list-of-companies-laying-off-employees-in-february-11445748\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's an overview of tech layoffs in 2026:\n\n## Tech Layoffs in 2026\n\n### Current Statistics\nSo far in 2026, there have been 28 layoffs at tech companies with 5,285 people impacted (294 people per day). January 2026 marked a painful milestone with nearly 25,000 job cuts across the tech sector.\n\nMore than 100 companies are planning to cut jobs in 2026, according to WARN Tracker's website.\n\n### Major Companies Announcing Layoffs\n\n**Amazon**\nTech giant Amazon plans to reduce its corporate workforce by 16,000 employees. This comes after reductions in October 2025 led to layoffs of 14,000 corporate employees. Together, the layoffs of 30,000 amount to about 10% of Amazon's corporate workforce.\n\n**Meta**\nMeta laid off approximately 1,500 employees from its Reality Labs division in January 2026, representing about 10% of that team's workforce. The cuts come after Reality Labs accumulated over $70 billion in losses since 2021. Meta is shifting focus away from its expensive metaverse obsession toward AI-powered wearables.\n\n**ASML**\nDutch chip-making equipment manufacturer ASML plans to lay off 1,700 employees, mainly affecting management roles in the company's IT and tech departments. The company plans to shift these management positions to more engineering roles in an effort to reduce corporate bureaucracy.\n\n**Dow**\nChemical maker Dow plans to lay off 4,500 employees, about 13% of its 36,000 employees. The layoffs, in addition to the use of AI and automation, are part of an initiative to simplify the company's operations.\n\n**Pinterest**\nPinterest will be slimming its workforce down by a little below 15 percent to put more emphasis on AI this year.\n\n**Nike**\nNike is cutting 775 jobs that will primarily impact jobs at the retailer's distribution centers in Tennessee and Mississippi as the company looks to automate more of its supply chain.\n\n### Key Drivers Behind Layoffs\nIn 2026, 55% of 1,000 U.S. hiring managers surveyed by Resume.org said they expect layoffs, and 44% anticipate that AI will be a top driver of layoffs.\n\nMuch of the downsizing reflects a shift in capital allocation as companies pour money into AI. Budgets are being redirected toward AI models, graphics processing units, cloud infrastructure and large-scale data centres. At the same time, automation tools are replacing some roles, forcing organisations to reshape their workforces.\n\nIn 2025, U.S. layoffs topped 1.1 million, with companies citing AI implementation, economic uncertainty, and cost-cutting as primary reasons for staff reductions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc19be83-c5d4-4d22-a1ea-f9f460ee03af","uuid":"d5cb9847-ba09-4df5-a6c6-3440b303124e","tool_use_result":{"query":"tech layoffs companies 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01MNgAhEjtyFCb3255xbCZpt","content":[{"title":"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | FOX 10 Phoenix","url":"https://www.fox10phoenix.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year"},{"title":"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | FOX 4 Dallas-Fort Worth","url":"https://www.fox4news.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year"},{"title":"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | FOX 11 Los Angeles","url":"https://www.foxla.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year"},{"title":"Layoffs Tracker - All Tech and Startup Layoffs","url":"https://www.trueup.io/layoffs"},{"title":"2026 tech company layoffs","url":"https://www.informationweek.com/it-staffing-careers/2026-tech-company-layoffs"},{"title":"Amazon’s layoffs are staggering. We’ve seen this before","url":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/31/tech/amazon-layoffs-january-tech-changes"},{"title":"Tech layoffs roundup: Every major company cutting jobs in 2025-2026","url":"https://www.androidheadlines.com/2026/01/tech-layoffs-roundup-every-major-company-cutting-jobs-in-2025-2026.html"},{"title":"One month into 2026, global tech and startup layoffs near 25,000","url":"https://www.businesstoday.in/technology/news/story/one-month-into-2026-global-tech-and-startup-layoffs-near-25000-514445-2026-02-03"},{"title":"2026 layoffs: List of companies cutting jobs this year | LiveNOW from FOX","url":"https://www.livenowfox.com/news/2026-layoffs-list-companies-cutting-jobs-year"},{"title":"List of companies laying off employees in February - Newsweek","url":"https://www.newsweek.com/list-of-companies-laying-off-employees-in-february-11445748"}]},"Based on the search results, here's an overview of tech layoffs in 2026:\n\n## Tech Layoffs in 2026\n\n### Current Statistics\nSo far in 2026, there have been 28 layoffs at tech companies with 5,285 people impacted (294 people per day). January 2026 marked a painful milestone with nearly 25,000 job cuts across the tech sector.\n\nMore than 100 companies are planning to cut jobs in 2026, according to WARN Tracker's website.\n\n### Major Companies Announcing Layoffs\n\n**Amazon**\nTech giant Amazon plans to reduce its corporate workforce by 16,000 employees. This comes after reductions in October 2025 led to layoffs of 14,000 corporate employees. Together, the layoffs of 30,000 amount to about 10% of Amazon's corporate workforce.\n\n**Meta**\nMeta laid off approximately 1,500 employees from its Reality Labs division in January 2026, representing about 10% of that team's workforce. The cuts come after Reality Labs accumulated over $70 billion in losses since 2021. Meta is shifting focus away from its expensive metaverse obsession toward AI-powered wearables.\n\n**ASML**\nDutch chip-making equipment manufacturer ASML plans to lay off 1,700 employees, mainly affecting management roles in the company's IT and tech departments. The company plans to shift these management positions to more engineering roles in an effort to reduce corporate bureaucracy.\n\n**Dow**\nChemical maker Dow plans to lay off 4,500 employees, about 13% of its 36,000 employees. The layoffs, in addition to the use of AI and automation, are part of an initiative to simplify the company's operations.\n\n**Pinterest**\nPinterest will be slimming its workforce down by a little below 15 percent to put more emphasis on AI this year.\n\n**Nike**\nNike is cutting 775 jobs that will primarily impact jobs at the retailer's distribution centers in Tennessee and Mississippi as the company looks to automate more of its supply chain.\n\n### Key Drivers Behind Layoffs\nIn 2026, 55% of 1,000 U.S. hiring managers surveyed by Resume.org said they expect layoffs, and 44% anticipate that AI will be a top driver of layoffs.\n\nMuch of the downsizing reflects a shift in capital allocation as companies pour money into AI. Budgets are being redirected toward AI models, graphics processing units, cloud infrastructure and large-scale data centres. At the same time, automation tools are replacing some roles, forcing organisations to reshape their workforces.\n\nIn 2025, U.S. layoffs topped 1.1 million, with companies citing AI implementation, economic uncertainty, and cost-cutting as primary reasons for staff reductions."],"durationSeconds":20.723714900000008}}
[2026-02-04T00:03:13.179Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FkhBPWxyLBdneaSsSsmCcf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Ukraine Russia conflict news February 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Russia-Ukraine war: List of key events, day 1,439 | Russia-Ukraine war News | Al Jazeera\",\"url\":\"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/2/russia-ukraine-war-list-of-key-events-day-1439\"},{\"title\":\"Trilateral Ukraine talks to resume in Abu Dhabi on Wednesday: Zelenskyy | Russia-Ukraine war News | Al Jazeera\",\"url\":\"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/1/trilateral-ukraine-talks-set-to-resume-in-abu-dhabi-on-wednesday-zelenskyy\"},{\"title\":\"Russo-Ukraine War - 02 February 2026\",\"url\":\"https://www.globalsecurity.org/military/world/war/russo-ukraine-2026-02-02.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Ukraine receives bodies of 1,000 soldiers from Russia | Russia-Ukraine war News | Al Jazeera\",\"url\":\"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/29/ukraine-receives-bodies-of-1000-soldiers-from-russia\"},{\"title\":\"Russia’s Grinding War in Ukraine | CSIS\",\"url\":\"https://www.csis.org/analysis/russias-grinding-war-ukraine\"},{\"title\":\"Eurasia Group | Russia’s second front: Eurasia Group's #5 Top Risk of 2026\",\"url\":\"https://www.eurasiagroup.net/live-post/risk-5-russias-second-front\"},{\"title\":\"Ukraine: Massive overnight attack leaves millions in the dark | UN News\",\"url\":\"https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/01/1166732\"},{\"title\":\"Russo-Ukraine War - 01 February 2026\",\"url\":\"https://www.globalsecurity.org/military/world/war/russo-ukraine-2026-02-01.htm\"},{\"title\":\"How might Russia's war on Ukraine change in 2026? - Atlantic Council\",\"url\":\"https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/event/how-might-russias-war-on-ukraine-change-in-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment, February 1, 2026 | Critical Threats\",\"url\":\"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/russian-offensive-campaign-assessment-february-1-2026\"}]\n\nHere is the latest news on the Ukraine-Russia conflict in February 2026:\n\n## Recent Developments\n\n### Ongoing Military Operations\nThese are key developments from day 1,439 of Russia's war on Ukraine. Since the beginning of the day on February 2, there have been 147 combat clashes. The Russian forces made 60 air strikes, dropped 150 controlled air bombs, used 5,199 kamikaze drones and carried out 2,860 shelling of settlements and positions of Ukrainian troops.\n\n### Civilian Casualties\nA Russian drone strike on a bus carrying miners in Ukraine's Dnipropetrovsk region killed at least 12 people. Ukrainian Deputy Prime Minister Denys Shmyhal denounced the strike as a \"cynical and targeted\" attack on energy workers. Their employer, DTEK, said the victims were finishing a shift.\n\nAnother Russian drone attack on the Ukrainian city of Dnipro killed a man and a woman, while nine people were wounded in Russian attacks on a maternity ward and a residential neighbourhood in Zaporizhzhia.\n\n### Peace Talks\nZelenskyy said a new round of trilateral talks between Russian, Ukrainian and US officials on a Washington-drafted plan to end the nearly four-year war has been postponed to February 4 and 5 in the United Arab Emirates capital, Abu Dhabi.\n\nThe first round of trilateral negotiations took place in late January but appeared to make little progress on the vital question of territory. Moscow still is demanding Kyiv cede a fifth of the Donetsk region that it still controls, which Zelenskyy's government is refusing to do.\n\nDmitry Medvedev, the deputy chairman of Russia's Security Council, praised US President Donald Trump's \"brash\" style as \"effective\" in seeking peace. Medvedev added that Trump \"wants to go down in history as a peacemaker – and he is really trying\", which explains \"why contacts with Americans have become much more productive\".\n\n### Energy Crisis\nNearly 700 apartment buildings remain without heating in the Ukrainian capital, Kyiv, due to previous Russian attacks on the country's energy infrastructure, as a new wave of bitter cold swept across much of the country.\n\n### Starlink Issue\nTech billionaire Elon Musk said moves by his SpaceX company to stop Russia's \"unauthorised\" use of its internet system Starlink seem to have worked, after Ukrainian officials reported finding Starlink terminals on long-range drones used in Russian attacks.\n\n### Casualty Figures\nSince February 2022, Russian forces have suffered nearly 1.2 million casualties, more losses than any major power in any war since World War II. At current rates, combined Russian and Ukrainian casualties could reach 2 million by the spring of 2026.\n\n### Body Exchange\nUkraine received the bodies of 1,000 soldiers from Russia in the latest exchange of those killed in the war. Russia and Ukraine confirmed the exchange as part of ongoing agreements reached earlier in the war to allow families to bury those killed on the battlefield.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc19be83-c5d4-4d22-a1ea-f9f460ee03af","uuid":"541f644e-2874-461b-9204-b21efc7c6ff2","tool_use_result":{"query":"Ukraine Russia conflict news February 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01CCzjWx5HGDYazc5zEn9LhP","content":[{"title":"Russia-Ukraine war: List of key events, day 1,439 | Russia-Ukraine war News | Al Jazeera","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/2/russia-ukraine-war-list-of-key-events-day-1439"},{"title":"Trilateral Ukraine talks to resume in Abu Dhabi on Wednesday: Zelenskyy | Russia-Ukraine war News | Al Jazeera","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/1/trilateral-ukraine-talks-set-to-resume-in-abu-dhabi-on-wednesday-zelenskyy"},{"title":"Russo-Ukraine War - 02 February 2026","url":"https://www.globalsecurity.org/military/world/war/russo-ukraine-2026-02-02.htm"},{"title":"Ukraine receives