new-era-us-drug-pricing"},{"title":"U.S. Drug Pricing Year in Review: Reflections on 2025 and Getting Ready for 2026 | Insights | Sidley Austin LLP","url":"https://www.sidley.com/en/insights/newsupdates/2025/12/us-drug-pricing-year-in-review-reflections-on-2025-and-getting-ready-for-2026"}]},"Here's what I found regarding pharmaceutical drug prices and related claims for 2026:\n\n## Drug Price Increases in 2026\n\nPharmaceutical companies have raised list prices for 872 brand-name medications, with a median increase of 4% in 2026. The number of price increases for 2026 is up from the same point last year, when drugmakers unveiled plans for raises on more than 250 drugs.\n\n### Despite Trump Administration Deals\n\nDrug companies, including the 16 that made deals with the Trump administration, raised the prices of 872 brand-name drugs in the first two weeks of 2026, according to an analysis by 46brooklyn. All 16 drug companies that inked deals with the Trump administration over the past few months still raised some of their prices for 2026.\n\nThe pricing deals struck between the Trump administration and drugmakers apply to the Medicaid program, while most Americans are on commercial health plans. MFN pricing could have a negligible impact on Medicaid patients because the program already guarantees the lowest price offered to any commercial payer.\n\n## Medicare Drug Price Negotiation Program\n\nFor the first time, the law provides Medicare the ability to directly negotiate the prices of certain high expenditure, single source drugs without generic or biosimilar competition. The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) selected ten drugs covered under Medicare Part D for the first cycle of negotiations for initial price applicability year 2026.\n\nThe first set of negotiated drug prices will go into effect in 2026 and are estimated to save $1.5 billion in annual out-of-pocket costs for Medicare beneficiaries while saving the Medicare program $6 billion per year.\n\nAfter the first round of negotiations in 2024, Medicare prices for 10 chosen drugs were reduced by 38% to 79%, with those discounts going into effect in January of 2026.\n\n## Specific Drug Price Changes\n\nPfizer announced the most increases, with hikes on about 80 drugs and vaccines. Most are below 10%, but Comirnaty, its COVID-19 vaccine, will rise by 15%.\n\nNot all changes are upward. Drugmakers will cut list prices on about nine medications, including a more than 40% reduction for Jardiance, a diabetes drug sold by Boehringer Ingelheim and Eli Lilly. Jardiance is among 10 drugs for which the U.S. government negotiated lower Medicare prices for 2026, forcing Boehringer and Lilly to slash its cost by two-thirds.\n\n## TrumpRx Direct-to-Consumer Platform\n\nThe Trump administration successfully enforced its \"most favored nation\" (MFN) policy on more than a dozen drugmakers this year, procuring agreements that ensure the companies will offer their products at MFN pricing. The direct-to-consumer platform called TrumpRx is expected to launch early in 2026.\n\n## Policy Outlook\n\n2026 kicks off a period when multiple effective dates converge, and policymakers are building the infrastructure to observe and enforce the interactions with more precision. Medicare negotiation, Inflation Reduction Act inflation-adjusted rebates, Part D redesign, 340B policy shifts, and new payment model tests are landing close enough together that they will start interacting in practice."],"durationSeconds":23.168256500000002}}
[2026-02-04T00:03:46.183Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019GJqpL9vjXyo4v91JNJ6HF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"immigration deportation controversy claims 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Judge blocks US gov’t move to end deportation protections for Ethiopians | Migration News | Al Jazeera\",\"url\":\"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/31/judge-blocks-us-govt-move-to-end-deportation-protections-for-ethiopians\"},{\"title\":\"Immigration Detention Is Harsher and Less Accountable Than Ever\",\"url\":\"https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/press-release/report-trump-immigration-detention-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"Weaponizing the System: One Year of Trump’s Attacks on Due Process | Vera Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.vera.org/explainers/weaponizing-the-system-one-year-of-trumps-attacks-on-due-process\"},{\"title\":\"His mistaken deportation was thought to be unique. But 'the problem is getting worse'\",\"url\":\"https://www.npr.org/2026/02/03/g-s1-108426/trump-kilmar-abrego-garcia-immigration-mistaken-deportations\"},{\"title\":\"Johnson Refutes Judicial Warrant Claim for Deportation Efforts\",\"url\":\"https://www.dailysignal.com/2026/02/03/johnson-refutes-judicial-warrant-claim-for-deportation-efforts/\"},{\"title\":\"Arrested and Deported U.S. Citizens and Permanent Residents: How the Media Lies to Vilify ICE | The Gateway Pundit | by Antonio Graceffo\",\"url\":\"https://www.thegatewaypundit.com/2026/02/arrested-deported-u-s-citizens-permanent-residents-how/\"},{\"title\":\"Immigration, mass deportations after one year under Trump\",\"url\":\"https://www.politifact.com/article/2026/jan/20/Trump-mass-deportations-1-year-immigration-ICE/\"},{\"title\":\"Deportation in the second Trump administration - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deportation_in_the_second_Trump_administration\"},{\"title\":\"Article: Unleashing Power in New Ways: Immigration.. | migrationpolicy.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.migrationpolicy.org/article/trump-2-immigration-1st-year\"},{\"title\":\"Kilmar Abrego Garcia has become a symbol of the pitfalls of immigration enforcement : NPR\",\"url\":\"https://www.npr.org/2026/02/02/nx-s1-5685162/kilmar-abrego-garcia-has-become-a-symbol-of-the-pitfalls-of-immigration-enforcement\"}]\n\nBased on my search, here's a summary of the key immigration deportation controversies and claims in 2026:\n\n## Major Controversies and Legal Challenges\n\n### Wrongful Deportations\nImmigration lawyers say Kilmar Abrego Garcia's landmark case highlights the challenges with the speed and scale of the Trump administration's goal of mass deportations. According to one of Abrego Garcia's lawyers, \"There have been countless illegal deportation cases since then. If anything, the problem is getting worse and not better.\" Abrego Garcia was deported to El Salvador even after an administrative judge agreed he had a credible fear of being tortured there.\n\n### TPS (Temporary Protected Status) Terminations\nA federal judge halted the Trump administration's plan to strip deportation protections from thousands of Ethiopians living in the United States. Judge Brian Murphy in Boston issued the order, delaying a February 13 deadline that would have forced more than 5,000 Ethiopians to leave the country or face arrest. Plaintiffs claim Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem acted based on an \"unconstitutional animus against non-white immigrants.\"\n\nA federal appeals court found the administration unlawfully ended protections for 600,000 Venezuelans. The three-judge panel said Noem's actions were based on \"racist stereotyping.\"\n\n### Detention System Expansion\nThe number of people held in ICE detention rose nearly 75 percent in 2025, climbing from roughly 40,000 at the start of the year to 66,000 by the start of December, the highest level ever recorded. By November 2025, for every person released from ICE detention, more than fourteen were deported directly from custody, compared to an approximate one-to-two ratio from a year earlier.\n\n### Due Process Concerns\nThe administration has planned to denaturalize certain U.S. citizens—setting a quota of up to 200 people per month in 2026—and sought to eliminate constitutionally protected birthright citizenship. People attending hearings and mandated check-ins with ICE are now being targeted for arrest. These arrests funnel people actively following the legal process into immigration detention, which advocates say undermines due process by punishing people for complying with the law.\n\n### Deaths in Detention\nOn January 3, 2026, a 55-year-old Cuban man died in ICE detention at Camp East Montana. ICE said his death was a suicide, but a witness said he was choked by a guard. Subsequently, the medical examiner ruled it a homicide. In January 2026, 46-year-old Cambodian refugee Parady La died from drug withdrawal in ICE detention in Philadelphia. La's family accused ICE of medical neglect.\n\n### Family Separations\nAdrian Conejo Arias and his five-year-old son, Liam, were apprehended by ICE in Minnesota in January 2026. The detention, documented in a photograph that went viral, gained significant attention as a representation of the increased deportation actions. A federal judge named Fred Biery ordered the release of the child and his father, denouncing the deportation policies as inhumane and illegal.\n\n### Warrant Debate\nSpeaker Johnson has pushed back on demands for judicial warrants for deportation efforts, saying \"There are some logic and reason that has to be applied here\" and claiming Democrats \"want to have a judicial warrant on top of the immigration judge's warrant.\"\n\n### Claims of U.S. Citizens Being Detained\nMedia reports cite 2025–2026 data claiming that more than 170 U.S. citizens were detained due to database errors or lack of immediate documentation. However, some conservative outlets dispute these claims and argue there is a distinction between detention and arrest.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc19be83-c5d4-4d22-a1ea-f9f460ee03af","uuid":"c7526052-e23a-454f-a057-7451c0002a07","tool_use_result":{"query":"immigration deportation controversy claims 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01NUbhZ3vD8qksGEhmkEGRMy","content":[{"title":"Judge blocks US gov’t move to end deportation protections for Ethiopians | Migration News | Al Jazeera","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/31/judge-blocks-us-govt-move-to-end-deportation-protections-for-ethiopians"},{"title":"Immigration Detention Is Harsher and Less Accountable Than Ever","url":"https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/press-release/report-trump-immigration-detention-2026/"},{"title":"Weaponizing the System: One Year of Trump’s Attacks on Due Process | Vera Institute","url":"https://www.vera.org/explainers/weaponizing-the-system-one-year-of-trumps-attacks-on-due-process"},{"title":"His mistaken deportation was thought to be unique. But 'the problem is getting worse'","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/02/03/g-s1-108426/trump-kilmar-abrego-garcia-immigration-mistaken-deportations"},{"title":"Johnson Refutes Judicial Warrant Claim for Deportation Efforts","url":"https://www.dailysignal.com/2026/02/03/johnson-refutes-judicial-warrant-claim-for-deportation-efforts/"},{"title":"Arrested and Deported U.S. Citizens and Permanent Residents: How the Media Lies to Vilify ICE | The Gateway Pundit | by Antonio Graceffo","url":"https://www.thegatewaypundit.com/2026/02/arrested-deported-u-s-citizens-permanent-residents-how/"},{"title":"Immigration, mass deportations after one year under Trump","url":"https://www.politifact.com/article/2026/jan/20/Trump-mass-deportations-1-year-immigration-ICE/"},{"title":"Deportation in the second Trump administration - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deportation_in_the_seco