rded. By November 2025, for every person released from ICE detention, more than fourteen were deported directly from custody, compared to an approximate one-to-two ratio from a year earlier.\n\n### Due Process Concerns\nThe administration has planned to denaturalize certain U.S. citizens—setting a quota of up to 200 people per month in 2026—and sought to eliminate constitutionally protected birthright citizenship. People attending hearings and mandated check-ins with ICE are now being targeted for arrest. These arrests funnel people actively following the legal process into immigration detention, which advocates say undermines due process by punishing people for complying with the law.\n\n### Deaths in Detention\nOn January 3, 2026, a 55-year-old Cuban man died in ICE detention at Camp East Montana. ICE said his death was a suicide, but a witness said he was choked by a guard. Subsequently, the medical examiner ruled it a homicide. In January 2026, 46-year-old Cambodian refugee Parady La died from drug withdrawal in ICE detention in Philadelphia. La's family accused ICE of medical neglect.\n\n### Family Separations\nAdrian Conejo Arias and his five-year-old son, Liam, were apprehended by ICE in Minnesota in January 2026. The detention, documented in a photograph that went viral, gained significant attention as a representation of the increased deportation actions. A federal judge named Fred Biery ordered the release of the child and his father, denouncing the deportation policies as inhumane and illegal.\n\n### Warrant Debate\nSpeaker Johnson has pushed back on demands for judicial warrants for deportation efforts, saying \"There are some logic and reason that has to be applied here\" and claiming Democrats \"want to have a judicial warrant on top of the immigration judge's warrant.\"\n\n### Claims of U.S. Citizens Being Detained\nMedia reports cite 2025–2026 data claiming that more than 170 U.S. citizens were detained due to database errors or lack of immediate documentation. However, some conservative outlets dispute these claims and argue there is a distinction between detention and arrest."],"durationSeconds":28.200000199999995}}
